Converting a duration to a number, on every target.

`unit duration { ms, s = 1000ms, m = 60s, h = 60m }` declares one base, so a
duration is a number of ms and `int(500ms)` is 500. JavaScript and Kotlin hold
it that way and always agreed. Go does not: a duration is a `time.Duration`
there, whose unit is nanoseconds, and the cast handed that straight out -- so
`int(d)` was 500000000 in Go and 500 on the other two, a disagreement between
targets inside one program rather than a number anyone could have chosen.

The representation is not the bug and does not change: `time.Duration` is what
a Go timer and the rest of the ecosystem want. Only the cast divides, which is
enough because a cast is the only place a duration leaves the representation.
Every other line below is arithmetic that stays inside it -- a sum with a
literal in a second suffix, a scale by a plain number, and a duration built by
multiplying the unit one -- and each is correct in ns right up to the cast that
divides it out. Read the Go column: the division lands outside the parentheses
the arithmetic already carries.

`float` divides as floats rather than converting after an integer division, so
a duration finer than a millisecond reports a fraction here as it does
elsewhere rather than truncating to zero.

deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `int\(m\.d__inst0\)` -- the bare cast is the bug: a time.Duration handed out as a number is nanoseconds
deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `float64\(m\.d__inst0\)\)` -- the same for the float cast, which must divide rather than widen
deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `int\(\(m\.d__inst0 \* 2\)\)` -- arithmetic reaches the cast still in ns, so a scaled duration has to divide too
broken out/go/bubbletea -- a duration times an int emits `m.one__inst0 * m.n__inst0`, mismatched time.Duration and int
-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import time "sngl:time"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
    none {
        html
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
}

component main ui.node {
    var d time.duration = 500ms
    var one time.duration = 1ms
    var n = 3

    ui.text(value="i {int(d)}")
    ui.text(value="f {float(d)}")
    ui.text(value="sum {int(d + 100ms)}")
    ui.text(value="secs {int(d + 1s)}")
    ui.text(value="scaled {int(d * 2)}")
    ui.text(value="built {int(one * n)}")
}

ui.window {
    main
}
